---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const pageTitle =
  '"Taipei": Come a-knocking';
const unit = '"Taipei"';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={pageTitle}
  unit={unit}
>
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-4">
    {pageTitle}
  </h1>
  <hr class="mb-6" />

  <!-- Scenario Explanation -->
  <details
    class="mb-4 border rounded p-4"
  >
    <summary
      class="cursor-pointer font-semibold"
    >
      Scenario: Understanding the
      Problem
    </summary>
    <p>
      A web server is running on <code
        >localhost:80</code
      >, but access is blocked by <strong
        >Port Knocking</strong
      >.
    </p>
    <ul
      class="list-disc list-inside mt-2 space-y-1"
    >
      <li>
        Only one knock is required (a
        single SYN packet to one port)
      </li>
      <li>
        No sequence — just one correct
        TCP port
      </li>
      <li>
        After knocking, <code
          >curl localhost</code
        > must work
      </li>
      <li>
        Root access is <strong
          >not</strong
        > available
      </li>
    </ul>
  </details>

  <!-- Concept Section -->
  <details
    class="mb-4 border rounded p-4"
  >
    <summary
      class="cursor-pointer font-semibold"
    >
      Core Concepts You Must Understand
    </summary>

    <h3 class="font-semibold mt-2">
      1. What is Port Knocking?
    </h3>
    <p>
      Port knocking is a firewall
      technique where access to a
      protected service (here: port 80)
      is granted only after a specific
      network packet is received.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-2">
      Typically implemented with:
    </p>
    <ul
      class="list-disc list-inside mt-2 space-y-1"
    >
      <li>
        <code>iptables</code> + recent module
      </li>
      <li><code>knockd</code></li>
      <li>Custom firewall logic</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="font-semibold mt-4">
      2. What is a SYN Packet?
    </h3>
    <p>
      TCP connections begin with a <strong
        >SYN</strong
      > packet (first step of the 3‑way handshake).
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2">
      The firewall listens for a SYN to
      a specific port. If correct, it
      temporarily allows access to port
      80.
    </p>

    <h3 class="font-semibold mt-4">
      3. Why Port 80 Looks Closed
    </h3>
    <p>
      The firewall blocks incoming
      traffic to port 80 until the knock
      is received. After knocking, a
      temporary rule allows access.
    </p>
  </details>

  <!-- Step 1 -->
  <details
    class="mb-4 border rounded p-4"
  >
    <summary
      class="cursor-pointer font-semibold"
    >
      Step 1: Confirm Port 80 is Blocked
    </summary>
    <pre
      class="bg-gray-100 p-2 rounded overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code>curl localhost</code></pre>
    <p>
      This should fail or hang,
      confirming the service is
      protected.
    </p>
  </details>

  <!-- Step 2 -->
  <details
    class="mb-4 border rounded p-4"
  >
    <summary
      class="cursor-pointer font-semibold"
    >
      Step 2: Scan All Ports
    </summary>
    <p>
      Since this is a single-port knock,
      one unusual open port must exist.
    </p>
    <pre
      class="bg-gray-100 p-2 rounded overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code>sudo nmap -p- -Pn localhost</code></pre>

    <p class="mt-2">Look for:</p>
    <ul
      class="list-disc list-inside mt-2 space-y-1"
    >
      <li>
        An uncommon high-numbered port
      </li>
      <li>Not 22, 80, 443</li>
      <li>
        Likely something unexpected
      </li>
    </ul>
  </details>

  <!-- Step 3 -->
  <details
    class="mb-4 border rounded p-4"
  >
    <summary
      class="cursor-pointer font-semibold"
    >
      Step 3: Send the SYN Knock
    </summary>

    <p>
      Suppose the open port found is <code
        >12345</code
      >. Send exactly one SYN:
    </p>

    <pre
      class="bg-gray-100 p-2 rounded overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code>sudo hping3 -S -p 12345 -c 1 localhost</code></pre>

    <p class="mt-2">
      This triggers the firewall rule
      allowing port 80 temporarily.
    </p>
  </details>

  <!-- Step 4 -->
  <details
    class="mb-4 border rounded p-4"
  >
    <summary
      class="cursor-pointer font-semibold"
    >
      Step 4: Access the Web Server
    </summary>

    <pre
      class="bg-gray-100 p-2 rounded overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code>curl localhost</code></pre>

    <p>Verify the md5:</p>

    <pre
      class="bg-gray-100 p-2 rounded overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code>echo $(curl localhost) | md5sum</code></pre>

    <p>
      Expected output:
      <code
        >fe474f8e1c29e9f412ed3b726369ab65</code
      >
    </p>
  </details>

  <!-- Firewall Mechanism -->
  <details
    class="mb-4 border rounded p-4"
  >
    <summary
      class="cursor-pointer font-semibold"
    >
      What Happens Behind the Scenes
    </summary>

    <p>Likely firewall logic:</p>

    <ul
      class="list-disc list-inside mt-2 space-y-1"
    >
      <li>Detect SYN to knock port</li>
      <li>
        Add source IP to allowed list
      </li>
      <li>
        Permit TCP traffic to port 80
      </li>
      <li>
        Timeout after short period
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="mt-2">
      This is often implemented with:
    </p>

    <pre
      class="bg-gray-100 p-2 rounded overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code>iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -m recent --rcheck --name knock -j ACCEPT</code></pre>
  </details>

  <!-- Persistence -->
  <details
    class="mb-4 border rounded p-4"
  >
    <summary
      class="cursor-pointer font-semibold"
    >
      Persistence Check
    </summary>
    <p>
      No configuration changes are made.
      This task is
      <strong>non-persistent</strong>.
    </p>
  </details>

  <!-- Key Takeaways -->
  <details
    class="mb-4 border rounded p-4"
  >
    <summary
      class="cursor-pointer font-semibold"
    >
      What You Must Remember
    </summary>

    <ul
      class="list-disc list-inside mt-2 space-y-1"
    >
      <li>
        Port knocking hides services
        behind firewall rules
      </li>
      <li>
        A SYN packet is enough to
        trigger access
      </li>
      <li>
        Scan all ports when behavior is
        unusual
      </li>
      <li>
        Act quickly before timeout
        expires
      </li>
      <li>
        Always validate using checksum
        if provided
      </li>
    </ul>
  </details>

  <!-- Command Dictionary -->
  <details
    class="mb-4 border rounded p-4"
  >
    <summary
      class="cursor-pointer font-semibold"
    >
      Command Dictionary
    </summary>

    <ul
      class="list-disc list-inside mt-2 space-y-1"
    >
      <li>
        <code
          >nmap -p- -Pn localhost</code
        > – Scans all 65535 TCP ports.
        <br />
        <code>-p-</code> = scan all ports
        <br />
        <code>-Pn</code> = skip host discovery
        (assume host is up)
      </li>

      <li>
        <code
          >hping3 -S -p 12345 -c 1
          localhost</code
        > – Sends crafted TCP packet.
        <br />
        <code>-S</code> = set SYN flag
        <br />
        <code>-p</code> = destination port
        <br />
        <code>-c 1</code> = send exactly one
        packet
      </li>

      <li>
        <code>curl localhost</code> – Performs
        HTTP request to local web server.
      </li>

      <li>
        <code>md5sum</code> – Calculates MD5
        checksum of input.
      </li>

      <li>
        <code>echo $(command)</code> – Executes
        command and passes output to echo
        (includes newline).
      </li>

      <li>
        <code>iptables</code> – Linux firewall
        utility.
        <br />
        <code>-A</code> = append rule
        <br />
        <code>-p</code> = protocol
        <br />
        <code>--dport</code> = destination
        port
        <br />
        <code>-m recent</code> = use recent
        module
      </li>
    </ul>
  </details>
</BaseLayout>
